LOGBOOK.md:416:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 settled `macos.provider` with **no keychain row and no file exception** because `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` grants `/Library/Keychains` read/write to any process holding `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`. Every observation was taken on **macOS 26.5 (25F71)**. The binding generated-target floor is **macOS 15.0** (`.spec/platform-distribution.md`, ADR-016/3r0993). A base sandbox grant is a property of the **running system**, not of this contract — so the matrix asserted, for nine major releases it never read, something true only of the one it did. Verdict 08 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:418:- FINDING: the floor is **not measurable in this environment** and that is reported, not worked around. No macOS 15 host, VM, installer or second volume; `tart`/`UTM`/`prlctl`/`VBoxManage` all absent; only Xcode 26.5 with macOS 26 SDKs — and sandbox profiles are **OS content, not SDK content** (`find` over `Xcode_26_5.app` returns no `application.sb`). Apple does not document the file. Verdict 08 explicitly allowed modelling instead.
LOGBOOK.md:419:- FINDING: installed products settle it in **neither** direction. Re-read on this Mac: Surfshark ships the `/Library/Keychains/` exception at `LSMinimumSystemVersion` **10.15**, bundled with `/private/var/db/mds/` and a third path; PureVPN's two providers carry `keychain-access-groups` at **10.15**; Tailscale's carries neither at **12.4**. Support ranges that wide isolate nothing about macOS 15.0. K1's clause runs **both ways** — a key present in a shipping bundle is not proof the mechanism functions, and a key absent from one is not proof it is unnecessary.
LOGBOOK.md:420:- DECISION: rule **K3** (`keychainSandboxFloorRule`), gate **R39**. K1 has two halves and only one is version-independent: `keychain-access-groups` is inapplicable on *every* macOS version (an access-control **model** — file-based keychain uses `SecAccess` ACLs, TN3137), while "the sandbox already grants what it needs" rests on a **policy file** that ships with the OS. Only the second is scoped. K1 gains `osFloorScope` (required by R29), the row gains `osVersionScope`, and its `reopensOnly` is **extended, not rewritten**.
LOGBOOK.md:421:- DECISION: rule **X1** gains a `conditionalExceptions` register — **reviewed but not granted** — holding pre-authorized entry **X1-C1** (`…temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write` = `/Library/Keychains/`, `macos.provider`, both macOS channels, `armed: false`). It is **disjoint from the active register by construction**, because R26's equality (active register == the exceptions the rows actually author) is what keeps the active one honest. Rationale: the spec bans an *unreviewed* exception, so one a supported OS may require must be reviewed **before** the evidence — otherwise X1 rejects the right answer the day it arrives.
